01a102ef-bf06-7663-b834-5cfcca76ce58	\N	mcq	<p>Some of my relatives have left ___ Canada ____ to find jobs.</p>	\N	<p>Some of my relatives have left for Canada hoping to find jobs. চাকরি পাবার আশায় আমার অনেক আত্মীয় কানাডা গিয়েছে ।<br /><br />The correct sentence is: "Some of my relatives have left for Canada hoping to find jobs."</p><ol><li><p>"for" is used to indicate the destination towards which someone or something is going. In this case, "Canada" is the destination, so "for" is the appropriate preposition.</p></li><li><p>"hoping" indicates the purpose or intention behind the action. The relatives are moving with the hope or expectation of finding jobs, hence "hoping to find jobs" is the appropriate phrase.</p></li></ol><p>The other options are incorrect due to the misuse of prepositions and the context of the sentence:</p><ul><li><p>"to, expectation" is incorrect because "to" is not the right preposition for indicating the destination in this context, and "expectation" does not fit grammatically.</p></li><li><p>"at, wishing" is incorrect as "at" is not used for indicating movement towards a destination in this context, and "wishing" doesn’t fit naturally in the sentence structure.</p></li><li><p>"for, desire" could be plausible, but "desire" is less commonly used in this context and is less specific than "hoping."</p></li></ul>	published	\N	27	2026-10-03 18:03:48.930581+00	2026-10-03 18:03:48.930581+00
