01a1030e-98cb-7545-abf2-065d444737e9	\N	mcq	<p>The tiny print on the poster is <u>___</u> small to read easily. Fill in the blank-</p>	\N	<p>The correct word to complete the sentence "The tiny print on the poster is ___ small to read easily" is "too."</p><p>Therefore, the complete sentence is: "The tiny print on the poster is too small to read easily."</p><p>Explanation:</p><ul><li><p><strong>"too" is used to indicate that something is in excess or more than what is desirable, suitable, or manageable. In this sentence, "too small" suggests that the size of the print is excessively small for easy reading.</strong></p></li><li><p>"so" is often used to introduce a result or effect and is usually followed by "that." For example, "The print is so small that it is hard to read." This construction is not present here.</p></li><li><p>"very" is used to emphasize the degree of something but does not necessarily imply that it's excessive or more than desired. "The print is very small" would suggest the print is quite small but doesn't inherently convey difficulty in reading.</p></li><li><p>"much" is not appropriate in this context, as it doesn't fit grammatically. "Much small" is not a standard construction in English.</p></li></ul>	published	\N	23	2026-10-03 18:37:30.752834+00	2026-10-03 18:37:30.752834+00
01a1030e-a03a-7165-90e5-a2cb9929e3e7	\N	mcq	<p>My father does not like terrorism and _____ .</p>	\N	<p>Negative agreement-এর নিয়মানুসারে উত্তর হবে - neither do I<br />The correct option is "neither do I."</p><p>Explanation:</p><p>The phrase "neither do I" is used to agree with a negative statement. In this case, the negative statement is "My father does not like terrorism." When agreeing with such a statement, "neither" is used followed by the auxiliary verb ("do" in this case) and the subject ("I"). This structure is called inversion, where the auxiliary verb precedes the subject in response to a negative statement.</p><ul><li><p>"Neither I do" - This is incorrect because the auxiliary verb and subject are not in the correct inverted order.</p></li><li><p>"I do" - This indicates agreement with a positive statement, which is not the case here.</p></li><li><p>"I do either" - This is grammatically incorrect. The correct negative agreement would be "I don't either," but "neither do I" is more formal and common.</p></li></ul>	published	\N	24	2026-10-03 18:37:32.656793+00	2026-10-03 18:37:32.656793+00
